rved in 5 of the 7 patients (71%25) evaluated. In subjects with more than one mutation%2C the response was seen in 2 of 3 patients (66%25);pmid=N/A;pubchem_cid=5328940;ref_id=25683327;ref_type=pubmedid;pgmd_reference_allele=T;pgmd_registry_identifiers=ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: NCT02228382;pgmd_relative_risk=N/A;rsid=rs121913461;pgmd_sample_size=30;pgmd_sex=Mixed;pgmd_site_genotype=C/C;pgmd_study_design=Clinical Trial%2C phase IV;pgmd_treatment_detail=Sample consists of chronic phase Philadelphia chromosome-positive chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) patients treated with bosutinib in fourth-line%2C after retrospectively reviewing 30 patients in chronic phase%2C and pretreated with imatinib%2C nilotinib%2C and dasatinib (one patient was also treated with ponatinib). With a median follow up of 11.1 months%2C the probability to either maintain or improve their CCyR response was 56.6%25 (17/30) and 11 patients (36.7%25) achieved or maintained their baseline MMR. All patients started bosutinib at 500 mg/day (median 400) and the dose was adjusted according to the clinician's judgment. Complete hematological response%2C Cytogenetic and Molecular responses were analyzed for a median follow up after bosutinib of 11.5 months. Median duration of treatment across all cohorts was 9.3 months;uniprot=P00519;pgmd_variant_class=missense;pgmd_variant_type=SNP;feature=>0 matches (T/C or C/C)-(G/A or A/A)-(A/T or T/T)-(C/G or G/G)-(G/A or A/A)-(T/C or C/C)-(T/G or G/G or T/A or A/A)-(A/G or G/G)-(G/T or T/T)-(G/C or C/C):Increased hematological response to bosutinib in TKI-resistant mutation carriers;hyperlink=https://portal.biobase-international.com/cgi-bin/knowledgebase/idb/1.0/get.cgi?HP000003614-410
